# Types

Opening fence
struct Glyph {
    unsigned int code_point = 0;   // sorted ascending in the index
    unsigned int x_offset = 0;     // ink left edge, pixels from the cell origin
    unsigned int width = 0;        // tight ink width in pixels
    unsigned int byte_offset = 0;  // offset into Font::data
    [[nodiscard]] constexpr unsigned int row_bytes() const;
};

struct Font {
    std::span<const std::byte> data;
    std::span<const Glyph> glyphs;
    unsigned int advance = 0;      // monospace cell width, pixels
    unsigned int height = 0;       // ascent + descent
    unsigned int ascent = 0;
    unsigned int line_height = 0;  // height plus the row gap the face wants
};

struct TextMetrics {
    unsigned int width = 0;
    unsigned int height = 0;
};
Closing fence

Font::data holds every glyph bitmap: per glyph, one bit per pixel, most
significant bit left, each row padded to a byte, rows top to bottom from the
ascent line. A glyph's rows begin at its byte_offset and are row_bytes()
wide. The index is sorted ascending by code point, is searched by binary
search, and leads with the space glyph at zero width.

# Renderer

Opening fence
[[nodiscard]] mm::display::Status render(
    const char8_t* text, std::size_t text_size, const Font& font,
    mm::display::Color foreground, mm::display::Color background,
    unsigned int x, unsigned int y,
    std::span<std::byte> frame, unsigned int frame_width_bytes);

[[nodiscard]] TextMetrics measure(const char8_t* text,
                                  std::size_t text_size, const Font& font);

[[nodiscard]] mm::display::Status expand_row(
    std::span<const std::byte> packed_row, unsigned int row_width_bytes,
    unsigned short foreground, unsigned short background,
    std::span<std::byte> rgb565_row);
Closing fence

render composes UTF-8 text into a one-bit packed frame in mm.display's
packing: row-major, most significant bit left, one white and zero black, byte
stride frame_width_bytes. Composition is transparent: glyph pixels take the
foreground, and every other pixel is left untouched, so text composes over
whatever is already in the frame. Polarity falls out of the bit convention:
white ink sets the glyph bits, black ink clears them. The background argument
is advisory; a caller that wants a cleared region clears it before rendering.

The host tests pin these semantics down:

- A code point absent from the font renders blank, and the pen still advances
  one cell for it.
- Text that reaches the right edge of the frame is clipped, not an error.
- render answers BadArgument for null text with a nonzero size, a zero frame
  stride, a frame shorter than its declared width, text reaching past the
  last frame row, a Red foreground, and any malformed UTF-8 sequence:
  truncated, overlong, surrogate, or out-of-range. measure is lenient by
  design: a malformed sequence ends the count, and nothing is measured past
  it.
- A glyph row is copied as whole bytes and rotated at its two edges, so the
  common case needs no per-pixel loop.

measure is the monospace contract: width is the count of well-formed code
points times the advance, and height is the font's line height. No per-glyph
scanning, so it costs nothing.

expand_row maps one packed one-bit row to RGB565 for the sixteen-bit panels,
most significant byte first: bit 1 becomes the foreground, bit 0 the
background, two bytes per pixel. A 240-pixel row is 480 bytes. It answers
BadArgument for a packed row shorter than its declared width or an output row
shorter than the expanded one.

# Glyph data

data.cppm carries the two committed tables. Both are generated from the
pinned IBMPlexMono-Regular.ttf at development time, and both hold the same
113 code points: the 95 printable ASCII plus the 18 Polish letters.

- kMono12: 113 glyphs, 1904 packed bytes, advance 7, height 17, ascent 13,
  line_height 17.
- kMono16: 113 glyphs, 4928 packed bytes, advance 10, height 22, ascent 17,
  line_height 22.

data.cppm is a generated file and must not be edited by hand; regenerating it
from the pinned TTF is the only supported change. Its header carries the
provenance the licence requires: the face, the TTF's SHA256, the charset, and
each table's glyph count and packed size, with the OFL attribution beside
licenses/OFL.txt.

Both tables fit both boards' flash with room for the rest of the project. At
the committed advances:

- 12 px, advance 7, line_height 17: 21 columns by 17 lines on the 152 by 296
  ePaper, 34 by 18 on the 240 by 320 LCD.
- 16 px, advance 10, line_height 22: 15 columns by 13 lines on the ePaper,
  24 by 14 on the LCD.

The runtime cost is negligible against refresh: a full ePaper text screen is
a write of 13 rows by 19 bytes against the panel's 200 ms class full refresh,
and nothing on the LCD, where refresh does nothing.

# Both panels from one sequence

A caller draws the same text on either panel with one portable sequence:
compose, write rows, refresh.

- Compose the lines into a one-bit frame a full panel row wide, centring each
  line with measure. Full rows satisfy the ePaper's write contract: x zero is
  a multiple of 8, and both panel widths, 152 and 240, are multiples of 8, so
  no row needs special handling.
- On a one-bit panel, write each row with mm.display::write as a rectangle of
  height one from the visible top-left origin.
- On a sixteen-bit panel, expand_row first maps each packed row to RGB565,
  most significant byte first, and write takes the expanded row.
- Refresh is always explicit and unchanged by this module.

apps/font-demo exercises exactly that sequence. It clears the panel white,
composes four centred lines, two at 16 pixels and two at 12, the last in the
Polish charset, holds them long enough to be read, and sleeps. It branches on
the panel's depth, and it names mm.fonts, mm.display, and mm.mcu, no board,
SDK, or vendor.

# Test strategy

The module's host tests under tests/mm/fonts exercise the pure font logic
without a display provider:

- The committed kMono12 and kMono16 index and data match a small golden set
  of glyph bitmaps, so a corrupted regeneration fails the suite.
- render of a known glyph at a known origin equals the expected packed bytes,
  in both polarities.
- Rendering over a pre-marked frame leaves the marked bits untouched in both
  polarities, the transparent composition contract.
- A missing code point renders blank and the pen still advances.
- measure obeys the monospace contract and counts multi-byte Polish letters
  one cell each.
- Text reaching the right edge is clipped rather than rejected.
- BadArgument: undersized frame, zero stride, text past the last row, Red
  foreground, truncated UTF-8, null text with a nonzero size.
- expand_row maps a known one-bit pattern to exact RGB565 bytes and rejects
  short input and output rows.

# Implementation stages

1. Implemented: add mm.fonts:types and mm.fonts:renderer with host tests.
2. Implemented: generate and commit the kMono12 and kMono16 IBM Plex Mono
   tables with their provenance header and the OFL text.
3. Implemented: add apps/font-demo drawing both sizes and the Polish line on
   both panel kinds.
4. Pending hardware: qualify legibility, polarity, alignment, and refresh on
   the named ePaper and LCD boards.

# Acceptance criteria

- The mm.fonts host tests pass without a display provider, Pico SDK, or
  hardware.
- No font table or renderer object appears in an executable that does not
  import mm.fonts.
- The same composed frame drives both panel kinds through mm.display alone.
- Full-row composition satisfies the ePaper's byte alignment on both panel
  widths.
- font-demo draws both committed sizes and the Polish line on a named display
  board before legibility is claimed.
- A successful build is not reported as legible text on a panel.
- The committed tables carry their provenance header, and the OFL text sits
  beside them in the repository.

# What this does not settle

- Antialiasing and grayscale are not implemented; both panels are one-bit or
  two-polarity in this project's contract.
- No 8 px table is committed. Plex Mono at 8 px is soft, the limit of a modern
  display face at an eight-pixel cell. An 8 px table would be a data question,
  another generated Font constant, not a design change.
- Weights, italics, and proportional metrics are not in the committed face or
  format; the data partition holds any number of tables if a face is wanted.
- RLE compression of glyph rows is deferred: the storage saving does not
  justify a decoder on either board's flash budget.
- Charset growth beyond printable ASCII and the 18 Polish letters is a
  regeneration, not a format change.
- Kerning, ligatures, and text shaping are out of scope; the face is
  monospace and the charset is a flat code-point set.
- Drawing primitives, image formats, and UI layout remain outside mm.fonts.
- No change reaches mm.display, mm.epaper, or mm.lcd; this module consumes
  only mm.display.
